.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt EPOLL 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm epoll_create ,
.Nm epoll_create1 ,
.Nm epoll_ctl ,
.Nm epoll_wait ,
.Nm epoll_pwait ,
.Nm epoll_pwait2
.Nd watch a set of descriptors
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/epoll.h
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_create1 "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_ctl "int epfd" "int op" "int fd" "struct epoll_event *event"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_wait "int epfd" "struct epoll_event *events" "int maxevents" "int timeout"
.Ft int
.Fn epoll_pwait "int epfd" "struct epoll_event *events" "int maxevents" "int timeout" "const sigset_t *sigmask"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An epoll set is a descriptor that keeps the descriptors a process
watches, so that a wait names them once rather than in every call, as
.Xr poll 2
does.
.Fn epoll_create1 ,
number 329 on i386 and 291 on x86-64, makes one;
.Fa flags
is 0 or
.Dv EPOLL_CLOEXEC .
.Fn epoll_create ,
number 254 on i386 and 213 on x86-64, is the older form, whose argument must be positive and is
otherwise ignored.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names the set
.Dq anon_inode:[eventpoll] .
.Pp
.Fn epoll_ctl ,
number 255 on i386 and 233 on x86-64, changes the set:
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
adds
.Fa fd
with the events in
.Fa event
and its word of data,
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
changes them, and
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_DEL
removes
.Fa fd .
The events are those of
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Dv EPOLLIN ,
.Dv EPOLLOUT ,
.Dv EPOLLRDHUP
and the rest;
.Dv EPOLLERR
and
.Dv EPOLLHUP
are always reported, for a registration of no events too.
A registration is level-triggered: a wait reports it while its
descriptor is ready.
With
.Dv EPOLLET
it is edge-triggered: a wait reports it when an event appears, or when
the descriptor has changed since its last report in a way its events
watch, as a pipe drained and filled again between two waits has, and not
again until then.
A change is data that came, which
.Dv EPOLLIN ,
.Dv EPOLLPRI ,
.Dv EPOLLRDNORM
and
.Dv EPOLLRDBAND
watch; room made to write, which
.Dv EPOLLOUT ,
.Dv EPOLLWRNORM
and
.Dv EPOLLWRBAND
watch; or a hangup, an error, a close or a shutdown, which every
registration watches.
So a socket watched for
.Dv EPOLLOUT
alone is not reported again when data comes to it, and one watched for
.Dv EPOLLIN
is reported for each arrival, as in Linux.
Each write to a pipe or a FIFO is a change of data for its read end,
the pipe empty before it or not, as in Linux.
With
.Dv EPOLLONESHOT
the first report disables it, errors and hangups included, until an
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD .
.Dv EPOLLEXCLUSIVE
is accepted at
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
and changes nothing, since each wait here wakes its own process.
.Pp
.Fn epoll_wait ,
number 256 on i386 and 232 on x86-64, waits until a descriptor of the set is ready, a signal
arrives, or
.Fa timeout
milliseconds pass, a negative
.Fa timeout
waiting without a limit, and stores at most
.Fa maxevents
events and their data in
.Fa events ;
with more ready than that, the next waits report the others first, so
each ready descriptor is reported in turn, as in Linux.
.Fn epoll_pwait ,
number 319 on i386 and 281 on x86-64, waits with the signal mask
.Fa sigmask
in place, as
.Xr ppoll 2
does.
.Fn epoll_pwait2 ,
number 441 on both, takes its timeout as a
.Vt struct timespec
of 64-bit seconds and nanoseconds; negative seconds are
.Er EINVAL ,
and a timeout longer than 2147483647 milliseconds, about 24.8 days, is
shortened to that.
A timeout ends at the first 10 ms tick at or after its end, and not
before
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
has advanced by it, as
.Xr poll 2
describes.
.Pp
A registration belongs to the descriptor number and the open file it
named when it was added, and lasts while that open file is open in any
process, as in Linux: a child of
.Xr fork 2
that closes its copy of the number leaves the registration to its
parent, and the wait after the open file's last close drops it.
A wait reports a registration only while the caller's descriptor at its
number names its open file, since a wait sleeps only on what the
caller's own descriptors keep open; Linux reports it through the open
file whatever became of the number.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr select 2
report a set readable while a wait on it would report an event: an
edge-triggered event already reported, or a one-shot registration that
is off, does not make it readable.
A set may be added to another: the outer set is ready while a wait on
the inner one would report an event, and a change in what the inner set
watches wakes a wait on the outer one, as in Linux.
Sets nest four deep at most below a set, a chain of five, as in Linux:
adding a set that would make a cycle, or a longer chain, fails with
.Er ELOOP ,
whether the chain would grow below the set added or above the set it is
added to, through every registration that lasts, whichever process made
it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn epoll_create1
returns the new descriptor,
.Fn epoll_ctl
0, and the waits the number of events stored, 0 when the time ran out;
each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EEXIST
.It Er EBADF
.Fa epfd
or
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er EEXIST
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
of a descriptor already in the set.
.It Er EFAULT
.Fa event
or
.Fa events
is not in the caller's memory.
A wait that cannot store an event returns the events it stored before
it, or this error when there are none; the events it could not store
are reported again by a later wait, one-shot and edge-triggered ones
among them, as in Linux.
.It Er EINTR
A signal arrived before a descriptor was ready.
.It Er EINVAL
.Fa epfd
is not an epoll set;
.Fa fd
is
.Fa epfd ,
or another descriptor of the same set;
.Fa op
or
.Fa flags
is not one of those above;
.Fa maxevents
is not positive;
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
touched
.Dv EPOLLEXCLUSIVE ;
or the timeout of
.Fn epoll_pwait2
has negative seconds, or nanoseconds outside 0 to 999999999.
.It Er ELOOP
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
of a set would make a cycle of sets, or a chain of more than five sets,
counting the sets that hold
.Fa epfd
as well as those that
.Fa fd
holds, whichever process made the registrations.
.It Er EMFILE
.Fn epoll_create
or
.Fn epoll_create1
found no free descriptor below the process's limit.
.It Er ENOENT
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_MOD
or
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_DEL
of a descriptor not in the set.
.It Er ENOMEM
The kernel heap has no room for a set, a larger descriptor table or an
open-file description, for a descriptor
.Dv EPOLL_CTL_ADD
registers, or for the events a wait collects.
.It Er EPERM
.Fa fd
is a regular file or a directory, which is always ready.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr eventfd 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr select 2
